---
description: C++ Formatting Rules: Line Length Limit (Max 60 Characters)
alwaysApply: true
---

Strictly enforce a maximum line length of 60 characters for code statements. When any statement exceeds 60 characters, break it according to the following rules:

# Rule 1: Split Variable Declaration / Assignment
If a standard assignment line exceeds 60 characters, break it immediately after the assignment operator (`=`):

Example:
// Exceeds 60 characters:
// FGameplayEffectContextHandle Context = AbilitySystemComponent->MakeEffectContext();

// Formatted:
FGameplayEffectContextHandle Context =
    AbilitySystemComponent->MakeEffectContext();

---

# Rule 2: Multi-line Subobject / Function Calls
If splitting after `=` still leaves the right-hand side (or second line) longer than 60 characters, split the method, template arguments, or parameter list across multiple indented lines:

Example:
AbilitySystemComponent =
    CreateDefaultSubobject<UAbilitySystemComponent>(
        TEXT("AbilitySystemComponent"));

---

# Rule 3: Function Parameters and Constructor Arguments
When function signatures, declarations, or calls exceed 60 characters due to multiple parameters or long arguments, place each argument on its own line:

Example - Function Calls:
// Exceeds 60 characters:
// ApplyGameplayEffectToTarget(EffectClass, TargetASC, Level, Context);

// Formatted:
ApplyGameplayEffectToTarget(
    EffectClass,
    TargetASC,
    Level,
    Context
);

Example - Function Declarations / Definitions:
void AMyCharacter::GrantAbility(
    TSubclassOf<UGameplayAbility> AbilityToGrant,
    int32 InputID,
    int32 Level
);

---

# Rule 4: Constructor Initializer Lists
If member initialization lists in constructors exceed 60 characters, align each initializer on a new line:

Example:
AMyActor::AMyActor()
    : PrimaryActorTick.bCanEverTick(true)
    , HealthComponent(CreateDefaultSubobject<UHealthComponent>(TEXT("HealthComp")))
{
}

---

## General Guidelines:
- Indentations: Use standard 4 spaces (or 1 tab) per nesting level for line continuations.
- Break points: Always break after `=`, opening parenthesis `(`, commas `,`, or template brackets `<` when lines exceed 60 characters.
- Consistency: Apply these rules strictly across all C++ header (`.h`) and source (`.cpp`) files in Unreal Engine projects.

---

# Rule 5: Include Order
The class header is the first include. It has no section comment.

Group every later include under a banner. Leave one blank line between groups. Omit an empty group. Keep this order:

```
// --- Engine ---
// --- GAME ---
// --- GAS ---
// --- Components ---
// --- Materials ---
// --- Helpers ---
// --- Net ---
```

Iris stays last, inside `#if UE_WITH_IRIS`, with no extra banner.

- **Engine**: Unreal headers that are not Components, Materials, Helpers, Net, or GAS. Sort the include paths.
- **GAME**: this project's headers outside `GAS/`. Core classes come first, in this order: GameMode, GameState, PlayerController, PlayerState, Pawn. Every other game class follows, sorted by include path.
- **GAS**: `AbilitySystem*`, `AttributeSet`, `GameplayEffect*`, `GameplayTag*`, `NativeGameplayTags`, and anything under `GAS/`. Engine GAS headers come first, then project headers. Sort each of those two groups by include path.
- **Components**: `Components/`. **Materials**: `Materials/`. **Helpers**: `UObject/ConstructorHelpers.h`. **Net**: `Net/` and `UObject/CoreNet.h`.

In a header, `CoreMinimal.h` stays first and `Class.generated.h` stays last. Neither gets a banner.